## 4. Backend Static Serving — One Express App, Three Roots

### The original bug (B3)

`server.js` had:

```js
app.use(express.static(frontendDir)); // mobile
app.use("/admin", express.static(adminDir)); // admin
app.get(/.*/, (req, res) => res.sendFile(frontendIndex)); // ← catches EVERYTHING
```

Because `app.get(/.*/)` (the mobile SPA fallback) came before a `/web`
mount that didn't exist, **any unknown non-API URL returned the mobile
`index.html`**. `/web/#/anime/123` returned the mobile shell, the
`/web/` folder had no deploy path, and a bad API path returned HTML
instead of JSON.

### The fix — mount order

The new `server.js` registers routes in this **exact order** (order matters —
the first matching handler wins):

```
1. security, parsers, CORS
2. all /api/*  routers (auth, anime, stream, etc.)
3. JSON 404 guard:  /^\/api(\/.*)?$/  →  { success:false, error:{code:'NOT_FOUND',...} }
4. /shared/client-contract  → static files
5. /admin            → static + SPA fallback → AdminDashboard/dashboard.html
6. ${WEB_MOUNT_PATH} (default /web) → static + SPA fallback → Web/index.html
7. /desktop-preview  (if SERVE_DESKTOP_PREVIEW) → static + Desktop/index.html
8. /  (mobile)       → express.static(Frontend)  [index:false]
9. errorHandler (global, AFTER all routes)
10. final catch-all  → Frontend/index.html   (only if SERVE_FRONTEND)
```

### Why `index: false`

Every `express.static` call sets `{ index: false }`. This prevents static
from auto-serving `index.html` on a directory request and lets the
explicit `app.get(webFallbackRegex, ...)` handler serve the SPA shell
instead — so `/web/anime/123` → SPA fallback → router picks the route.

### Cache headers

A shared `setStaticHeaders` function sets:

- `*.html` → `no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate` (always fresh)
- Hashed assets (`*-[hash].js`, `*.css`) → `public, max-age=31536000, immutable`

### API 404 guard (the JSON-not-HTML fix)

Right after the `/api/*` routers but BEFORE any static serving:

```js
const api404 = (req, res) =>
  sendError(res, 404, {
    code: "NOT_FOUND",
    message: "API endpoint not found.",
    requestId: req.id,
  });
app.use("/api", api404);
```

So `GET /api/does-not-exist` returns JSON, never the mobile HTML shell,
which previously masked API 404s as "the app returned an unreadable
response".

