## 6. Per-Client Settings Resolution (B7)

### The problem

The old code had:

```js
const resetPath = clientAgnostic.PASSWORD_RESET_PATH; // single global value
```

With mobile + web + desktop alive, a password-reset email can only ever
return to ONE client. Mobile users get sent to `/reset-password`, web users
to `/reset-password`, etc. — all pointing to the same place.

### The fix — how `getPasswordResetPath(client, requestedPath)` works

```js
// 1. Parse allow-list from env RESET_PATHS_JSON =
//   JSON.stringify({"mobile":"reset","web":"reset","desktop":"reset","admin":"reset"})
// If a caller requests a path via ?resetPath=foo, it's only used if "foo" is in the allow-list.

// 2. Per-client map from env GOOGLE_RETURN_TARGETS_JSON:
//   {"mobile":"anistrim://auth","web":"https://anistrim.com/reset","...":"desktop":"anistrim-desktop://reset"}

const getPasswordResetPath = (client, requestedPath) => {
  if (requestedPath && RESET_PATH_ALLOW_LIST.has(requestedPath))
    return requestedPath;
  if (resetPaths[client]) return resetPaths[client];
  return resetPaths.mobile; // fallback
};
```

Rules:

- **`client`** comes from `req.headers['x-client']` (default `'mobile'`).
- **`requestedPath`** from `req.query.resetPath` — accepted ONLY if in the
  strict `RESET_PATH_ALLOW_LIST` set. Otherwise ignored and the
  per-client default is used.
- Never reflect a caller-supplied URL — only values that passed the
  allow-list reach the email link.

`getGoogleReturnTarget(client, requestedTarget)` has the identical pattern
using `GOOGLE_RETURN_TARGETS_JSON` + `GOOGLE_RETURN_ALLOW_LIST`.

### Where it's called

- `controllers/authController.js` `forgotPassword` — resolves the reset path.
- `controllers/googleAuthController.js` `googleCallback` (the `?client=api`
  branch) — resolves the deep link.

### Google redirect URIs

The prompt instructs: **register every Google redirect URI you use** in the
Google Cloud Console. Currently the only one is:
`${BACKEND_URL}/api/auth/google/callback`. Any new client that uses the
GIS (Google Identity Services) redirect flow must have its URI pre-registered.

