## 7. Token Storage & Session Isolation (B8)

### The problem

- Mobile app uses keys: `token`, `refresh_token`, `session_token`.
- Web app uses keys: `web_token`, `web_refresh_token`.
- If Web/ is served from the same origin as Frontend/ (e.g. at `/web`),
  both share the same `localStorage`. A user could be logged in on mobile
  and logged out on web (or vice versa) because the apps read/write
  different key names.

### The fix — how `session.js` works

```js
AniStrimSession.create("mobile"); // → prefix 'anistrim.mobile.'
AniStrimSession.create("web"); // → prefix 'anistrim.web.'
AniStrimSession.create("desktop"); // → prefix 'anistrim.desktop.'

// Keys written:
localStorage["anistrim.mobile.token"]; // mobile access token
localStorage["anistrim.web.token"]; // web access token
localStorage["anistrim.desktop.token"]; // desktop access token
```

Each client reads **only its own prefixed keys**, so two sessions on the
same origin never collide.

### Legacy migration (backward compatibility)

On the **first read** of a token, if the prefixed key is missing, the
session module checks the legacy keys:

- `token` / `refresh_token` → migrates to `anistrim.{client}.token` / `.refreshToken`
- `web_token` / `web_refresh_token` → migrates to `anistrim.web.token` / `.refreshToken`

After migration the legacy key is **cleared** so there's only ever one
canonical key set per client. This lets existing logged-in users stay
logged in when the code updates.

### Desktop specifics

Electron `file://` pages send **no Origin header**, so they are already
allowed by the no-origin branch of the CORS origin function. No wildcard
CORS is needed or added — the Desktop app connects to the same API base
with `X-Client: desktop` and its scoped token keys.

