## 10. Request Flow Examples (Walkthrough)

### Flow A: Mobile app fetches anime detail (the common case)

```
1.  Mobile Capacitor WebView
    → fetch('https://anistrimbackend.onrender.com/api/anime/123',
       headers: { 'Authorization': 'Bearer <mobile token>',
                  'X-Client': 'mobile' })
       NOTE: no CapacitorHttp engine involved — standard browser fetch.

2.  CORS middleware (config/cors.js)
    → Origin = capacitor://localhost
    → isOriginAllowed → YES (in allow-list, env-independent)
    → sets Access-Control-Allow-Origin: capacitor://localhost
    → credentials: false, so no cookie headers

3.  server.js API 404 guard
    → /api/anime/123 doesn't hit the 404 catch (animeRoutes handles it)

4.  controllers/animeController.js
    → queries DB, returns sendSuccess(res, { id, title, ... })

5.  Mobile fetch receives response
    → res.json() = { success: true, data: { ... } }
    → AniStrimEnvelope.unwrap → { ok: true, data: {...} }
```

### Flow B: Web browser deep-link /web/anime/123

```
1.  User navigates to https://anistrim.com/web/anime/123
    (or clicks a link inside the Web SPA)

2.  server.js static serving chain
    → doesn't match /api/...
    → doesn't match /shared/...
    → doesn't match /admin/...
    → matches /web/ mount (WEB_MOUNT_PATH = '/web')
    → express.static(Web/) looks for /web/anime/123 → NOT a real file
    → falls through to SPA fallback regex → serves Web/index.html

3.  Browser loads index.html → router.js reads URL
    → route = /anime/123 → calls api.js → GET /api/anime/123

4.  Same API flow as Flow A, but with X-Client: web
    and token keys prefixed anistrim.web.*

5.  Browser gets { success:true, data:{...} }
    → Web ui.js renders the anime detail page.
```

### Flow C: Forgotten password on desktop

```
1.  User clicks "forgot password" in the Electron app.

2.  Frontend calls POST /api/auth/forgot-password
    headers: { 'X-Client': 'desktop' }
    body: { email: 'user@example.com' }

3.  authController.forgotPassword
    → client = req.headers['x-client'] = 'desktop'
    → resetPath = getPasswordResetPath('desktop', undefined)
                 = resetPaths['desktop']   // e.g. 'reset'
                 = 'reset'

4.  In dev mode (NODE_ENV !== 'production'):
    → response includes resetUrl = 'https://anistrim.com/reset?token=<jwt>'

5.  User clicks link → desktop browser or web browser
    → token is verified server-side, new password set.
```

### Flow D: Unknown API path

```
1.  fetch('https://anistrimbackend.onrender.com/api/no-such-route')

2.  server.js: matches /api/* → hits api404 middleware FIRST
    → returns JSON { success:false, error:{ code:'NOT_FOUND', ... } }

3.  Client unwrap → { ok: false, error: { code, message, status, requestId } }
    → http.js throws ApiError or the caller handles res.ok === false
```

### Flow E: CORS rejection (new observability)

```
1.  fetch('https://anistrimbackend.onrender.com/api/anime/1')
    Origin: https://evil-site.com

2.  config/cors.js origin()
    → origin NOT in allowed set
    → console.warn('[cors] blocked origin', 'https://evil-site.com')
    → callback(null, false) → browser blocks the response

3.  Developer sees the console.warn in server logs → can immediately
    diagnose "someone is calling the API from a non-allow-listed origin."
```

